(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-e6-l37-38-study-focus-set-2"]={"title":"Lectures 37–38 Study Focus — Set 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man has 12 mm of induration on a tuberculin skin test. His chest radiograph shows a fibrotic upper-lobe lesion, he does not recall ever being treated for tuberculosis, and he has no symptoms. Which step must come before latent-infection treatment?","choices":["An interferon-gamma release assay","Exclusion of active tuberculosis disease","A repeat skin test in 8 to 10 weeks","A second chest radiograph in 1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrotic lesions warrant review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease, and tuberculosis disease must be excluded before latent-infection treatment begins. Imaging cannot reliably determine activity, so microbiologic testing answers that question. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29, 37"},{"q":"Why is an interferon-gamma release assay preferred over the tuberculin skin test for most BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin)-vaccinated patients?","choices":["It separates latent from active disease","Its cut-offs are adjusted for vaccine history","It is not made positive by the vaccine","The vaccine makes skin tests falsely negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccination does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay, which measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens, whereas the vaccine can cause a false-positive skin test. The assay is therefore preferred for most vaccinated patients. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 24, 21, 19"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman shared an office with a coworker diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 2 weeks ago, and her exposure has now ended. She has no symptoms and no immune problem, and her interferon-gamma release assay today is negative. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Begin latent-infection treatment","Repeat the test in 8 to 10 weeks","No further testing is needed","Three sputum specimens today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. She is symptom-free and immunocompetent, so no immediate disease evaluation is triggered. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has no risk for resistant organisms, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 30%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Azithromycin as a single drug","Cefpodoxime plus azithromycin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no comorbidities and no resistant-organism risk, the outpatient choices are amoxicillin or doxycycline. Macrolide monotherapy is used only if local pneumococcal resistance is below 25%, and hers is 30%, so amoxicillin fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man is admitted with cough, night sweats and weight loss, and his sputum smear shows acid-fast bacilli. Infectious pulmonary tuberculosis is presumed. Where should he be placed?","choices":["Hallway bed until cultures return","Contact precautions with gown and gloves","Shared ward with standard precautions","Airborne infection isolation room"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis calls for an airborne infection isolation room when indicated, with fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection for staff, because pulmonary disease releases infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 33"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with alcoholism and chronic liver disease has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin in the past. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline as a single drug","Amoxicillin as a single drug","Amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcoholism and chronic liver disease are listed comorbidities, so he needs a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). His amoxicillin anaphylaxis removes the amoxicillin-based combination, leaving the fluoroquinolone, with a review of QT, interactions and fluoroquinolone risks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man starts treatment for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease and asks how long he will need it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["6 months after symptoms resolve","12 months past culture conversion","12 months from the first dose","Until his chest imaging is normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment continues for at least 12 months after culture conversion, so the clock starts when cultures turn negative, not with the first dose. That makes the whole course longer than 12 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure is hospitalized on day 5 of influenza, confirmed by molecular testing, with hypoxemia. Which antiviral is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral or enteric oseltamivir","Inhaled zanamivir","Single-dose baloxavir","Amantadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients generally receive oral or enteric oseltamivir, which is preferred for hospitalized, severe and high-risk patients and is started even after 48 hours. His age, lung and heart disease and admission all point to it, with renal dose adjustment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 98, 108, 109"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has chronic cough, sputum, fatigue and weight loss. Computed tomography shows fibrocavitary disease, alternative diagnoses have been excluded, and her sputum cultures are negative, but one bronchoalveolar lavage culture grows Mycobacterium avium complex. Which best describes whether she meets the diagnostic criteria?","choices":["Not met; a biopsy is required","Met on imaging alone","Not met; two sputum cultures needed","Met; one positive lavage qualifies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nontuberculous lung disease requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging (fibrocavitary disease here), exclusion of alternatives, and microbiology. One positive bronchial wash or lavage can meet the microbiologic criterion, even when sputum is negative. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who takes long-term prednisone has bronchoscopy to evaluate a lung nodule. A Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction test on the lavage fluid is positive at a low level. She has no dyspnea, fever or cough, her oxygen saturation is normal on room air, and her imaging shows no infiltrates. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["It calls for adjunctive corticosteroids","It must be repeated on expectorated sputum","It may reflect colonization, not infection","It confirms Pneumocystis pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction is highly sensitive but can detect colonization, so it is interpreted with the symptoms, the imaging and the organism burden. A low-level positive in a woman with no symptoms, a normal oxygen saturation and no infiltrates does not establish pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 62, 65, 67, 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with no immune problems developed fever, chills, a nonproductive cough and chest pain after clearing soil under a bird roost, and her urine Histoplasma antigen is positive. Five weeks later she is no better: her cough and fatigue are worsening, but she is not hypoxemic and is managing at home. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Continued observation","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Amphotericin-based induction","Oral itraconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasmosis is treated by severity and host risk. Her illness is prolonged and progressive but not severe, so itraconazole is considered, with monitoring of interactions and drug levels; observation fits mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult, and amphotericin is for severe disease. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 76, 77, 78, 123"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with acute bronchitis was managed with supportive care 10 days ago and at first improved. She now returns with a new temperature of 102.2°F, shortness of breath and crackles at the left base. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph for pneumonia","Codeine cough syrup","Reassurance about cough length","Empiric bronchitis antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Worsening after initial improvement, fever and dyspnea are return precautions, and fever or focal findings prompt evaluation for pneumonia rather than empiric bronchitis antibiotics. A worsening cough means reconsidering the diagnosis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with chest radiography. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 20, 29"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has had a sudden fever, myalgias, a frontal headache and a dry cough for one day, at the peak of a busy influenza season. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude influenza","It rules out influenza","It points to a bacterial cause","She is no longer contagious"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid antigen tests have limited sensitivity, and a negative result does not exclude influenza, especially during high community activity. Her sudden typical illness at the peak of the season keeps influenza likely; molecular testing confirms it when the result affects management. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection has a tuberculin skin test read at 48 hours, showing 6 mm of transverse induration. She has no cough, fever or weight loss. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start latent-infection treatment now","Repeat the skin test in 8 to 10 weeks","No action; below the 10 mm cut-off","Evaluate for active tuberculosis disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In human immunodeficiency virus infection the skin test cut-off is 5 mm, so 6 mm is positive. A positive test shows infection, not whether it is latent or active, so clinical evaluation and chest imaging come next to rule out active disease before any preventive therapy. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 22, 41"},{"q":"A 70-year-old unvaccinated man with diabetes and heart failure has mild coronavirus disease 2019 that began 6 days ago. His oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Molnupiravir","A 3-day remdesivir course","Dexamethasone","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk outpatient with mild or moderate disease and no hypoxemia is offered nirmatrelvir/ritonavir within 5 days or a three-day intravenous remdesivir course within 7 days of symptom onset. At day 6 only the remdesivir window is still open, and his age, comorbidities and lack of vaccination make him high risk. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 113, 120, 122"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man gets hives whenever he eats eggs and asks whether he can receive this season's influenza vaccine. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine. What is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Skip influenza vaccination","An egg-free product","Vaccinate under specialist supervision","Routine vaccination and safety steps"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Egg allergy does not require an egg-free product or precautions beyond routine vaccine safety measures. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine, so he receives an age- and health-appropriate vaccine this season like any other adult. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 6"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with no chronic conditions received the 15-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine 14 months ago at a pharmacy and has had no pneumococcal vaccine since. Which plan completes her pneumococcal vaccination?","choices":["A second 15-valent conjugate dose","A 23-valent dose when she turns 65","A 23-valent polysaccharide dose now","Nothing; her series is complete"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every adult aged 50 or older is eligible, and a series started with the 15-valent conjugate vaccine usually needs the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine afterward, usually 1 year or more later. Fourteen months have passed, so she gets it now. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia has a Pneumonia Severity Index total of 82 points. After intravenous fluids she remains hypotensive and needs a vasopressor infusion to maintain her blood pressure. She is breathing on her own. Where should she be treated?","choices":["Intensive care unit-level care","A general ward, by her score","An observation unit for a repeat score","Outpatient care with close follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasopressor-dependent shock is one of the severity signals of severe community-acquired pneumonia, with mechanical ventilation or at least three minor criteria, and severe pneumonia needs urgent intensive care-level assessment and circulatory support. The Pneumonia Severity Index guides the outpatient-or-ward choice only in nonsevere pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 30, 45"},{"q":"What does a positive acid-fast smear of sputum establish?","choices":["Drug susceptibility of the organism","An immune response to infection","Confirmed Mycobacterium tuberculosis","A group of organisms, not the species"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, so culture and molecular testing must name the species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 26"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted 5 days ago for a hip fracture develops fever, purulent sputum and a new infiltrate. She is not intubated and has no risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, no resistance risk and no high mortality risk. The hospital antibiogram shows most local Pseudomonas isolates are resistant to piperacillin-tazobactam but susceptible to cefepime. Which empiric agent is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefepime","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Cefepime plus vancomycin","Piperacillin-tazobactam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonia that begins 48 hours or more after admission is hospital-acquired. With no major resistance or high mortality risk, one antipseudomonal agent with methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus coverage is chosen using the local antibiogram, and hers favors cefepime over piperacillin-tazobactam. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 42, 44, 46"},{"q":"For suspected Pneumocystis pneumonia, which respiratory specimen is obtained first when it is feasible?","choices":["Spontaneously expectorated sputum","Lung biopsy tissue","Induced sputum","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Induced sputum is obtained for microscopy and/or polymerase chain reaction when feasible; if it is negative or unavailable and suspicion remains high, bronchoalveolar lavage follows. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 65, 66, 67, 69"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with ventilator-associated pneumonia was started on cefepime plus vancomycin because methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. On day 3 her respiratory culture grows Pseudomonas susceptible to cefepime, and no Staphylococcus aureus grows. She is improving. What is the most appropriate change?","choices":["Add a second antipseudomonal class","Continue both drugs for 14 days","Stop vancomycin, continue cefepime","Switch to moxifloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her culture shows cefepime-susceptible Pseudomonas and no Staphylococcus aureus, and she is improving, so therapy is de-escalated to the culture and susceptibility results: the vancomycin added for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is stopped and cefepime continues, usually for 7 days in total. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 46"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman's roommate was diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 10 weeks ago, and they have lived apart since. She has no symptoms. Her tuberculin skin test, read at 72 hours, shows 7 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; any measurable induration counts","Positive; recent close contacts use 5 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A recent close contact of infectious tuberculosis is in the 5 mm group, with human immunodeficiency virus infection, transplant, major immunosuppression and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. 7 mm is at least 5 mm, so the test is positive and she needs evaluation for active disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22, 41"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman with dysphagia after a stroke lives at home. She has 3 days of fever, a productive cough and a new right lower lobe infiltrate, and imaging shows no abscess or empyema. She is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere pneumonia. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated and has had no recent intravenous antibiotics. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefepime plus vancomycin","Supportive care, no antibiotic","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Weeks of ampicillin-sulbactam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever, productive cough and an infiltrate in a patient at risk of aspiration is aspiration pneumonia, an infection treated with the regimen for its setting and severity: here nonsevere pneumonia from home on a ward, a beta-lactam plus a macrolide. With no abscess or empyema, anaerobic coverage is not added. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 50, 51, 56"},{"q":"In Pneumocystis pneumonia associated with human immunodeficiency virus, which room-air arterial oxygen pressure indicates adjunctive corticosteroids?","choices":["Below 50 mm Hg","Below 60 mm Hg","Below 70 mm Hg","Below 80 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated when the room-air arterial oxygen pressure is below 70 mm Hg, started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of starting Pneumocystis therapy. Evidence outside the virus is less certain. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man has 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, and pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected. Which sputum collection plan is appropriate?","choices":["Three samples 8 to 24 hours apart, 1 early morning","Two samples, collected 1 hour apart in clinic","Three samples, 1 each week for 3 weeks","One sample, collected at any time of day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Suspected pulmonary tuberculosis calls for 3 sputum specimens collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen, sent for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the role of antibiotics in uncomplicated acute bronchitis?","choices":["Needed after 3 weeks of cough","Avoided in routine cases","Started empirically for fever","Started for colored sputum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Routine antibiotics are not prescribed for uncomplicated acute bronchitis, regardless of cough duration or sputum color, because they do not improve the viral cough illness. Their harms include adverse effects, C. difficile infection and resistance. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 18, 126"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with no condition that increases his risk of severe respiratory syncytial virus disease asks whether he should get the respiratory syncytial virus vaccine this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["Not yet; one dose is due at 75","Yes, one dose every year","Yes, two doses 4 weeks apart","Yes, one dose now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One respiratory syncytial virus vaccine dose is recommended for all adults 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 who are at increased risk of severe disease. At 66 with no such risk he is not yet eligible; when he turns 75, one dose is recommended, not a yearly one. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"Which agent provides coverage for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus in pneumonia?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Linezolid","Ceftriaxone","Ertapenem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage is indicated, vancomycin or linezolid is used. It is added only for validated risk or local prevalence, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 46"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who races pigeons has 6 days of fever, severe headache and a dry cough, with a lower-lobe infiltrate. A respiratory nucleic acid test detects Chlamydia psittaci. He has no drug allergies. Which treatment is preferred?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Doxycycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bird exposure, severe headache, a dry cough and a lower-lobe infiltrate with Chlamydia psittaci on a respiratory nucleic acid test is psittacosis. Doxycycline is preferred in adults, started promptly and continued about 10 to 14 days after the fever resolves to prevent relapse. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 43, 71, 81, 85, 88"}]};
